## Solution

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains no leaked answer, solution sketch, or formalization notes. Proceeding.

**Setup.** We have the complete graph $K_{36}$ on 36 hubs. Each edge is assigned one of 5 colors (corporations). A "Supply Chain Link" is an ordered triple $(A,B,C)$ of three **distinct** hubs such that the edges $AB$ and $BC$ receive the same color. (If $A=C$ were allowed, the triple $(A,B,A)$ would be trivially counted since $AB$ and $BA$ are the same edge, making the problem degenerate; hence $A,B,C$ are distinct.)

**Counting.** For a fixed central hub $B$ and a fixed color $c$, let $d_c(B)$ denote the number of edges incident to $B$ colored $c$. The number of ordered triples $(A,B,C)$ with center $B$ and color $c$ is $d_c(B)\bigl(d_c(B)-1\bigr)$, since $A$ and $C$ each range independently over the $d_c(B)$ neighbors of $B$ in color $c$, with $A\neq C$.

The total number of Supply Chain Links is
$$T=\sum_{B}\sum_{c=1}^{5}d_c(B)\bigl(d_c(B)-1\bigr)=\sum_{B}\left(\sum_{c=1}^{5}d_c(B)^2-\sum_{c=1}^{5}d_c(B)\right).$$

Since every hub $B$ has degree $35$ in $K_{36}$, we have $\sum_{c=1}^{5}d_c(B)=35$, so
$$T=\sum_{B}\left(\sum_{c=1}^{5}d_c(B)^2-35\right).$$

**Lower bound.** By the convexity of $x^2$ (or the QM–AM / Cauchy–Schwarz inequality), for each fixed $B$ the sum $\sum_{c}d_c(B)^2$ is minimized when the five values $d_c(B)$ are as equal as possible. Since $35=5\times 7$, the minimum is attained at $d_c(B)=7$ for all $c$:
$$\sum_{c=1}^{5}d_c(B)^2\ge 5\cdot 7^2=245.$$
Hence for each $B$,
$$\sum_{c=1}^{5}d_c(B)\bigl(d_c(B)-1\bigr)\ge 245-35=210,$$
and summing over all 36 hubs,
$$T\ge 36\times 210=7560.$$

**Achievability (the bound is tight).** It remains to exhibit a 5-coloring in which every hub has degree exactly $7$ in every color, giving $T=7560$.

The complete graph $K_{36}=K_{2\cdot 18}$ admits a **1-factorization**: its edge set decomposes into $35$ perfect matchings (1-factors), each consisting of $18$ disjoint edges. (This is the standard factorization of $K_{2n}$ into $2n-1$ perfect matchings.)

Partition these $35$ perfect matchings into $5$ groups of $7$ matchings each. Assign one color to each group. Then each color class is the union of $7$ perfect matchings, which is a $7$-regular graph on $36$ vertices. Consequently every hub $B$ satisfies $d_c(B)=7$ for all five colors $c$, achieving
$$T=36\times(5\cdot 7^2-35)=36\times 210=7560.$$

**Conclusion.** The minimum possible number of Supply Chain Links over all 5-colorings of $K_{36}$ is exactly $7560$. Therefore the largest $k$ guaranteed for every distribution is

$$\boxed{k=7560}.$$

